<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1453–1515</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as El Gran Capitán, the Great Captain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gonzalo Fernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, known as the Great Captain, was a Castilian general who fought in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and then commanded Spanish armies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Combining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a>, arquebusiers and fortified positions, he defeated the French at Cerignola and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garigliano">Garigliano</a> in 1503, winning the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a>, and served as its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> until 1507. His methods shaped the later Spanish infantry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gonzalo-fernandez-de-cordoba.jpg" alt="Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eduardo Carrió · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_gran_capit%C3%A1n_(Museo_del_Prado).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
